BRANCH = {
 "SignatureFoundations": "A digital signature is the proof that the person who controls a private key has authorized a transaction, and this branch collects what the chapter's first sections say about it: the three jobs of a signature (authorization by the owner, non-repudiation and integrity of the signed data), the numbers it is computed with, and the fact that each input of a transaction carries its own independent signature. It matters because every payment on the blockchain stands or falls with a signature check, and the check needs only the message, the public key and the signature. The branch is verified by running the chapter's implementation of ECDSA and schnorr, built on the curve arithmetic of chapter 4, and by comparing the results with Bitcoin Core (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).",
 "SighashFlags": "A signature does not have to cover the whole transaction: the SIGHASH flag, one byte at the end of the signature, selects which inputs and outputs the signed hash commits to, and this lets several people build one transaction together, for example a crowdfunding payment to which strangers add inputs. This branch collects the flag values, what each flag commits to (found by changing one field at a time), the known pitfall of SIGHASH_SINGLE and the agreement of the chapter's implementation with Bitcoin Core's 500 test vectors and with signatures that Bitcoin Core 31.1 made with every flag (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026).",
 "SchnorrSignatures": "Schnorr signatures, introduced to Bitcoin by the taproot soft fork of November 2021 as BIP340, are the newer of the two signature algorithms of the chapter and the one whose mathematics is simplest: a three-move proof of knowledge of a private key, turned into a signature by replacing the verifier's challenge with a hash. This branch walks from the toy protocol with ordinary integers to the 64-byte format of BIP340, then to the consequences of the algebra that the book highlights, namely the linearity that allows batch verification, scriptless multisignatures that look like one signature, and threshold signatures built on Shamir's secret sharing. Each step is computed, and the format is checked against the 19 official test vectors (Wuille et al., 2020).",
 "EcdsaSignatures": "ECDSA is the signature algorithm that Bitcoin used from its first release and that it still uses for every output that is not a taproot output. This branch gives its equations, shows the malleability that results from its form, explains the DER serialization of the book's example (itself a real signature from the blockchain) and separates what the network enforces as consensus from what a node only relays as policy. The statements are tested on the book's own sample, on signatures made by the chapter's library and on the answers of Bitcoin Core 31.1 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Wuille, 2015).",
 "Randomness": "The nonce of a signature must be unpredictable to everyone and used once, because with a repeated or biased nonce the private key can be computed from the signatures; this branch shows the algebra of the recovery, the standard remedy of deriving the nonce deterministically from the key and the message (RFC 6979 for ECDSA and the default algorithm of BIP340 for schnorr), what Bitcoin Core does in practice (deterministic nonces, grinding for a small R, zero auxiliary data for taproot) and what that does to the size of signatures. Every behaviour is read from the software's source and confirmed by running it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Pornin, 2013).",
 "SegwitSigning": "Segregated witness changed how the data that is signed is built, and this branch collects the two consequences that the chapter's last section and the later BIPs describe: the cost of verifying a transaction with many inputs, which grew with the square of the number of inputs in the old algorithm and grows linearly in the BIP143 algorithm, and the commitment of the signature to the amount of the coin being spent (and, in BIP341, to all the amounts), which the old algorithm did not give. It ends with taproot signing, where the chapter checks a real key-path spend and the verdicts of Bitcoin Core on malformed schnorr signatures (Lau and Wuille, 2016) (Wuille et al., 2020).",
 "SinceTheBook": "The 3rd edition went to print in 2023 and some of its statements have moved: proposals have changed status, Bitcoin Core has added support for MuSig2 and a limit on signature operations, a threshold signature BIP has been proposed, the library has changed an interface, and two new drafts concern ECDSA and the quantum threat. This branch collects those changes with their sources, adds the dates of the history the chapter touches (the patent, the book's real transaction, the taproot activation) and records how the evidence of the package was gathered, so that each statement can be traced to a saved copy of its source (Bitcoin Core, 2026) (Bitcoin Optech, 2026).",
}

